    21	
    22	## 1. System Architecture & Library Stack
    23	
    24	The evaluation stack is composed of three cooperating libraries:
    25	
    26	| Package | Role in the Competition Stack |
    27	| :--- | :--- |
    28	| **`adk-submission`** | **Declarative Agent Compiler & Inference Server Manager.** Compiles untrusted submission directories (`agent.yaml`, prompts, sub-agents, skills, and PEFT/LoRA adapters) into live Google ADK `BaseAgent` trees (`compile_submission`) without executing arbitrary competitor Python code (`importlib` is never used). Also manages the local `VllmServer` and `TransformersServer` processes. |
    29	| **`adk-eval-core`** | **Core Evaluation Infrastructure.** Provides base task/result models (`BenchmarkTask`, `BaseTaskResult`, `EvaluationResult`), sandbox execution backends (`DockerContainer`, `SubprocessSandbox`), resilient 3-tier file editing (`apply_replacement`), token/cost tracking (`TokenBudget`, `PricingTable`), ADK plugins (`EventDisplayPlugin`, `ModelRetryPlugin`), and ATIF v1.7 trajectory tracing (`SessionTrace`). |
    30	| **`swegemma`** | **SWE-bench Harness & Scoring Engine.** Implements the two-container SWE evaluation lifecycle (`Evaluator`, `run_agent_sandbox`, `verify_task`), binds the 9 sandboxed workspace and code-intelligence tools (`SwegemmaContext.create_tools()`), constructs the task prompt and continuation nudges, extracts unified git diffs (`scripts/inference.py`), and runs hermetic `pytest` + JUnit XML verification (`scripts/metric.py`). |
    31	
    32	```mermaid
    33	flowchart TB
    34	    subgraph Submission["Competitor Submission (submission.zip / agent_dir)"]
    35	        YAML["agent.yaml + sub_agents/*.yaml"]
    36	        EvalCfg["eval_config.yaml (Optional Budgets)"]
    37	        Prompts["prompts/*.md, configs/*.yaml"]
    38	        Adapters["adapters/* (Optional LoRA / Weights)"]
    39	    end
    40	
    41	    subgraph Host["Harness Process (swegemma + adk-submission + adk-eval-core)"]
    42	        Validator["validate_directory & validate_single_declared_model"]
    43	        Server["Local Inference Server (vLLM / Transformers :8000)"]
    44	        Compiler["compile_submission() -> ADK Runner"]
    45	        Tools["SwegemmaContext (9 Bound Tools + Budget Gate)"]
    46	    end
    47	
    48	    subgraph Sandboxes["Isolated Per-Task Sandboxes"]
    49	        ContA["Container A: Agent Sandbox (/workspace)\nSnapshot + Editable Install + Baseline Commit"]
    50	        ContB["Container B: Verification Sandbox (/workspace)\nFresh Snapshot + agent_patch + test_patch + pytest"]
    51	    end
    52	
    53	    Submission --> Validator
    54	    EvalCfg --> Host
    55	    Adapters --> Server
    56	    Validator --> Compiler
    57	    Server <-->|"OpenAI-compatible /v1 API"| Compiler
    58	    Compiler <-->|"Tool Calls & JSON Responses"| Tools
    59	    Tools <-->|"docker exec / subprocess"| ContA
    60	    ContA -->|"git add -N . && git diff --binary"| ContB
    61	    ContB -->|"exit_code == 0 & JUnit XML valid"| Score["Resolution Rate [0.0, 1.0]"]
    62	```
    63	
    64	---
    65	
    66	## 2. Submission Contract & Declarative Agent Schema (`adk-submission`)
    67	
    68	### 2.1. Declarative-Only Security Model
    69	Competitors **do not** submit Python code entrypoints (`agent.py` or `agent_fn`). Standard ADK's `from_config()` permits arbitrary dynamic Python imports; **`adk-submission` replaces it with a sandboxed YAML compiler (`compile_submission`)**. All agents, sub-agents, workflows, tools, and generation parameters are declared in YAML and resolved against closed host registries (`ToolRegistry`, `ModelRegistry`, `SkillRegistry`, `CallbackRegistry`).
    70	
    71	### 2.2. Submission Directory Layout
    72	A valid submission directory (packaged at the root of `submission.zip`, evaluated by `scripts/inference.py` in Stage 1 to produce `submission.parquet`) follows this layout:
    73	
    74	```text
    75	submission/
    76	├── agent.yaml                  # REQUIRED: Root agent config (or root_agent.yaml; .yml also accepted)
    77	├── eval_config.yaml            # Optional: Per-task evaluation budget & timeout overrides
    78	├── configs/
    79	│   └── sampling.yaml           # Optional: Generation parameters loaded via !include
    80	├── prompts/
    81	│   ├── system.md               # Optional: System instructions loaded via !include
    82	│   └── analyzer.md
    83	├── sub_agents/
    84	│   └── code_analyzer.yaml      # Optional: Sub-agent or AgentTool YAML configurations
    85	├── adapters/                   # Optional: Fine-tuned PEFT LoRA adapters or model weights
    86	│   ├── main_lora/
    87	│   │   ├── adapter_config.json
    88	│   │   └── adapter_model.safetensors
    89	│   └── tool_lora/
    90	│       ├── adapter_config.json
    91	│       └── adapter_model.safetensors
    92	└── skills/                     # Optional: ADK Skill directories (each containing SKILL.md)
    93	    └── repo_navigation/
    94	        └── SKILL.md
    95	```
    96	
    97	- **Root Config Discovery**: Exactly one root config (`agent.yaml`, `agent.yml`, `root_agent.yaml`, or `root_agent.yml`) must exist in the submission root. Having zero root files raises `MissingRootConfigError`; having more than one raises `MultipleRootConfigsError`.
    98	- **Optional Evaluation Budget Config (`eval_config.yaml`)**: Submissions may include an optional `eval_config.yaml` at the root under an `evaluation:` key (`timeout_seconds`, `max_tool_calls`, `max_time_minutes`, `max_turns`) to customize per-task operational budgets during Stage 1 inference (`load_submission_eval_config` in `scripts/inference.py`).
    99	- **Sandboxed `!include` Directive**: YAML files may embed external files using `!include <relative_path>`:
   100	  - `.md` and `.txt` files are loaded as raw UTF-8 strings (ideal for `instruction` or `global_instruction`).
   101	  - `.yaml` and `.yml` files are parsed recursively (up to a maximum include depth of `10` with cycle detection).
   102	  - Absolute paths, null bytes, `..` traversal components, and symlinks resolving outside the submission root are strictly blocked (`PathTraversalError`).
   103	
   104	### 2.3. Supported Agent Classes (`agent_class`)
   105	`SandboxedAgentConfig` supports four discriminated agent types (defaulting to `LlmAgent` if `agent_class` is omitted):
   106	
   107	1. **`LlmAgent` (default)**:
   108	   - `name` (`str`, required): Agent identifier string.
   109	   - `model` (`str`, required unless a default model is set): Model alias from the registry (`gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, `gemma-4-12b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, `gemma-4-e4b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, or `gemma-4-e2b-it-qat-w4a16-ct` for competition submissions).
   110	   - `adapter` (`str | None`): Optional adapter name matching a subdirectory or weight file under `adapters/`.
   111	   - `description` (`str`, up to `1,000,000` chars): Used by parent agents when delegating to this agent.
   112	   - `instruction` (`str`, up to `1,000,000` chars): System prompt for the agent. Supports ADK session state templating (`{problem_description}`, `{hints}`, or custom `output_key` variables).
   113	   - `tools` (`list`): List of tool names (`str`), `AgentTool` references (`agent_tool: {config_path: ..., skip_summarization: true}`), or inline agent dictionaries.
   114	   - `skills` (`list[str] | None`): List of relative paths to skill directories within the submission archive.
   115	   - `sub_agents` (`list`): List of `{config_path: ...}` references for multi-agent transfer.
   116	   - `output_key` (`str | None`): Saves the agent's final text output into `session.state[output_key]`.
   117	   - `include_contents` (`"default" | "none"`): Controls whether conversation history is passed to the agent.
   118	   - `disallow_transfer_to_parent` / `disallow_transfer_to_peers` (`bool | None`).
   119	   - `generate_content_config` (`dict | None`): Sampling, seed, and thinking parameters (see constraints below).
   120	2. **`SequentialAgent`**:
   121	   - Executes `sub_agents` in sequential order. Accepts `name`, `description`, `sub_agents`, and agent callbacks.
   122	3. **`ParallelAgent`**:
   123	   - Executes `sub_agents` concurrently in isolated branches. Accepts `name`, `description`, `sub_agents`, and agent callbacks.
   124	4. **`LoopAgent`**:
   125	   - Repeatedly executes `sub_agents` up to `max_iterations` (`int`, default `500`, bounded to `1..500`).
   126	
   127	### 2.4. Enforced Structural Limits & Generation Constraints
   128	Submission constraints in `swegemma` (`build_submission_limits()` in `swegemma/config.py`) are intentionally minimal: **aside from security sandboxing (no symlinks, no path traversal, declarative YAML only), the primary hard constraint is a total unpacked submission size of `< 3 GiB` (`3,221,225,472` bytes), including all `adapters/` weights**, plus token ceilings required by the 4x L4 GPU context window (`32,768` tokens).
   129	
   130	All other structural limits are set to generous safety ceilings solely to guard against runaway recursion or zip/YAML anchor bombs:
   131	
   132	| Category | Parameter | Competition Limit / Allowed Range |
   133	| :--- | :--- | :--- |
   134	| **Primary Archive Constraint** | **`max_total_size_bytes`** | **`3 GiB` (`3,221,225,472` bytes total unpacked size, including `adapters/`)** |
   135	| **Structural Safety Ceilings (`SubmissionLimits`)** | `max_file_count` | `10,000` files |
   136	| | `max_yaml_files` | `1,000` YAML files |
   137	| | `max_yaml_size_bytes` | `50 MiB` per YAML file / cumulative `!include` expansion |
   138	| | `max_skill_size_bytes` | `50 MiB` per skill directory |
   139	| | `max_instruction_chars` | `1,000,000` chars per agent (`10,000,000` total across all agents) |
   140	| | `max_agents` | `500` total agents in the compiled tree |
   141	| | `max_sub_agent_depth` | `50` levels of nesting (`sub_agents` and `agent_tool`) |
   142	| | `max_skills` | `1,000` skills |
   143	| | `max_loop_iterations` | `500` iterations for any `LoopAgent` |
   144	| | `allowed_file_extensions` | Strictly required formats only: `.yaml`, `.yml` (agent/eval configs), `.md`, `.txt` (prompts & `SKILL.md`), `.py` (ADK skill scripts), `.json` (`adapter_config.json`), and `.safetensors` (`adapter_model.safetensors`). Pickle-based weights (`.bin`, `.pt`, `.pth`) and other binary/archive extensions are rejected. |
   145	| | `adapter_extensions` | `.safetensors` only (`adapters/<name>/adapter_model.safetensors`) |
   146	| **Generation Config (`GenerationConstraints`)** | `allowed_fields` | **All schema fields permitted**: `temperature`, `top_p`, `top_k`, `max_output_tokens`, `presence_penalty`, `frequency_penalty`, `stop_sequences`, `response_mime_type`, `seed`, `thinking_config` |
   147	| | `max_output_tokens` | `1` to `32,768` (default `16,384`; bounded by 4x L4 vLLM `max_model_len=32768`) |
   148	| | `thinking_config.thinking_budget` | `0` to `32,768` (default `4,096`; bounded by 4x L4 vLLM `max_model_len=32768`; positive values set `enable_thinking: true` and forward `extra_body.thinking_token_budget` to vLLM's `ThinkingTokenBudgetLogitsProcessor`, while `0` sets `enable_thinking: false`) |
   149	| | `thinking_config.thinking_level` | `"MINIMAL"`, `"LOW"`, `"MEDIUM"`, `"HIGH"`, or `"NONE"` (case-insensitive). *Note*: When serving via vLLM's OpenAI-compatible `/v1` endpoint (especially with LoRA adapters), omit `thinking_level` and configure `include_thoughts: true` + `thinking_budget: 4096` instead, because `thinking_level` maps to OpenAI's `reasoning_effort` parameter (`swegemma` also sets `litellm.drop_params = True`). |
   150	| | `thinking_config.include_thoughts` | `true` or `false`. When `thinking_budget > 0` (or `thinking_level` is active), `include_thoughts: true` retains `Part(thought=True)` in `LlmResponse` and subsequent tool-call context (`<\|channel>thought...<channel\|>`), whereas `include_thoughts: false` still enables reasoning on the current turn (`enable_thinking: true`) while stripping `Part(thought=True)` from `LlmResponse` and outgoing message history. When neither `thinking_budget` nor `thinking_level` is set, `include_thoughts` acts as a fallback toggle for `enable_thinking`. |
   151	| | `temperature` / `top_p` / `top_k` / penalties / `seed` / `stop_sequences` | Unrestricted (any valid schema value; `temperature >= 0.0`, `0.0 <= top_p <= 1.0`, `top_k >= 1`; `seed` is forwarded in the outgoing request body to LiteLLM/vLLM) |
   152	
   153	> [!WARNING]
   154	> Execution-altering ADK fields that bypass the sandbox (`tools`, `system_instruction`, `http_options`, `safety_settings`, or `response_schema` inside `generate_content_config`) are excluded from `GenerateContentConfig` by schema design and will raise a validation error if set.
   155	
   156	---
   157	
   158	## 3. Model Registry, Single-Model Rule & LoRA Adapters on 4x L4 GPUs
   159	
   160	### 3.1. Computational Environment: 4x NVIDIA L4 GPUs (96 GB Total VRAM)
   161	The competition evaluation environment runs on a dedicated machine equipped with **4 × NVIDIA L4 GPUs**:
   162	- **Per-GPU Memory**: `24 GB GDDR6` (`96 GB` total VRAM across 4 GPUs).
   163	- **vLLM Serving Configuration (`setup_vllm_server` in `scripts/inference.py`)**:
   164	  - `tensor_parallel_size = 4` (shards model weights and KV cache across all 4 L4 GPUs)
   165	  - `gpu_memory_utilization = 0.90` (`~21.6 GB` usable per GPU / `~86.4 GB` usable across 4 GPUs)
   166	  - `max_model_len = 32768` (`32,768` tokens maximum combined prompt + reasoning + output context length)
   167	  - `scheduler_reserve_full_isl = False`
   168	  - `enable_auto_tool_choice = True`, `tool_call_parser = "gemma4"`, `reasoning_parser = "gemma4"`, `reasoning_config = {"reasoning_start_str": "<|channel>", "reasoning_end_str": "<channel|>"}`
   169	  - Dynamic LoRA right-sizing from discovered adapters: `enable_lora = bool(adapter_map)`, `max_loras = max(1, len(adapter_map))`, `max_lora_rank = max(detected_ranks) if detected_ranks else 128`
   170	  - Model-specific CLI flags (`MODEL_VLLM_EXTRA_ARGS`): for `gemma-4-12b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, passes `--hf-overrides '{"architectures": ["Gemma4ForCausalLM"], "model_type": "gemma4"}'`
   171	
   172	| Base Model Variant | Weight Precision | Approximate Weight Footprint (4 GPUs) | Competition Status |
   173	| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
   174	| **`gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`** *(Starter Kit Default)* | W4A16 (INT4 Quantized) | `~16–18 GB` (`~4.5 GB / GPU`) | **Permitted in Kaggle competition submissions** (`ALLOWED_MODEL_NAMES`) |
   175	| **`gemma-4-12b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`** | W4A16 (INT4 Quantized) | `~6–8 GB` (`~1.8 GB / GPU`) | **Permitted in Kaggle competition submissions** (`ALLOWED_MODEL_NAMES`) |
   176	| **`gemma-4-e4b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`** | W4A16 (INT4 Quantized) | `~3–4 GB` (`~1.0 GB / GPU`) | **Permitted in Kaggle competition submissions** (`ALLOWED_MODEL_NAMES`) |
   177	| **`gemma-4-e2b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`** | W4A16 (INT4 Quantized) | `~2–3 GB` (`~0.7 GB / GPU`) | **Permitted in Kaggle competition submissions** (`ALLOWED_MODEL_NAMES`) |
   178	| **`gemma-4-31b-it` / `gemma-4-27b-it` / `gemma-4-26b-a4b-it` / `gemma-4-12b-it` / `gemma-4-9b-it` / `gemma-4-e4b-it` / `gemma-4-e2b-it`** | `bfloat16` | `~4–62 GB` | Registered in `models.yaml` / `_build_cli_model_registry()` for local CLI evaluation only |
   179	
   180	This 4x L4 hardware budget directly governs four operational rules:
   181	1. **Single Base Model Per Submission (`ALLOWED_MODEL_NAMES`)**: All agents in a competition submission must share a single declared base model from the four official quantized models (`gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, `gemma-4-12b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, `gemma-4-e4b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, or `gemma-4-e2b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`).
   182	2. **Context Window Ceiling (`32,768` tokens)**: `max_output_tokens` and `thinking_budget` cannot exceed `32,768` tokens, matching vLLM's `max_model_len=32768`.
   183	3. **Total Unpacked Submission Size (`< 3 GiB` including `adapters/`)**: Ensures rapid extraction into `/kaggle/working/submission` and guarantees that submitted LoRA adapters fit within host disk and GPU LoRA buffers (`max_lora_rank <= 128`).
   184	4. **Host Container Sandboxing (`4 GiB` RAM / `2 vCPUs` per container)**: Prevents repository test suites inside `Container A` and `Container B` from starving the host CPU or system RAM needed by the vLLM engine.
   185	
   186	### 3.2. The Single Base Model Rule
   187	Before launching the inference server, `validate_single_declared_model(agent_dir)` (`scripts/inference.py`, wrapping `adk_submission.discovery.discover_declared_models`) traverses the root `agent.yaml`, every referenced `sub_agents[*].config_path`, every `tools[*].agent_tool.config_path`, and any standalone agent YAML files in the submission directory.
   188	- Provider prefixes (`openai/`, `google/`, `hosted_vllm/`, `custom/`) are stripped (`normalize_model_name`).
   189	- **All agents in a submission must declare at most ONE unique base model** (`len(models) == 1`), and in Kaggle competition scoring (`scripts/inference.py`) that model must be in `ALLOWED_MODEL_NAMES` (`gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, `gemma-4-12b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, `gemma-4-e4b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, or `gemma-4-e2b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`). Declaring multiple base models or an unpermitted model raises `ParticipantVisibleError`.
   190	
   191	### 3.3. Pre-Registered Model Aliases & Routing
   192	`models.yaml` and `_build_cli_model_registry()` (`src/swegemma/cli.py`) register the following Gemma 4 aliases in `ModelRegistry`:
   193	- `gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, `gemma-4-12b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, `gemma-4-e4b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, `gemma-4-e2b-it-qat-w4a16-ct` (Official quantized competition models)
   194	- `gemma-4-31b-it`, `gemma-4-31b`
   195	- `gemma-4-27b-it`, `gemma-4-27b`
   196	- `gemma-4-26b-a4b-it`, `gemma-4-26b-a4b`, `diffusiongemma-26b-a4b-it`
   197	- `gemma-4-12b-it`, `gemma-4-12b`
   198	- `gemma-4-9b-it`, `gemma-4-9b`
   199	- `gemma-4-e4b-it`, `gemma-4-e4b`
   200	- `gemma-4-e2b-it`, `gemma-4-e2b`
   201	
   202	When the scoring harness starts the local inference server (`setup_vllm_server` in `scripts/inference.py`, running `VllmServer` on `127.0.0.1:8000` with `tool_call_parser='gemma4'`, `reasoning_parser='gemma4'`, `max_model_len=32768`), it binds the declared model alias (and any discovered adapter names) to `LiteLlm(model=f"openai/{served_model}", api_base="http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1")` via `server_instance.create_model_registry(...)` (or fallback `LiteLlm(..., num_retries=3)` when attaching to an already-active endpoint) and installs the LiteLLM reasoning patch (`install_litellm_reasoning_patch()`).
   203	
   204	### 3.4. Multi-LoRA Serving (`adapters/`) & Sizing Guidelines
   205	Even though a submission is restricted to a single base model, **different agents in your hierarchy can use different fine-tuned LoRA adapters**:
   206	1. Place PEFT LoRA directories (containing `adapter_config.json` and `adapter_model.safetensors`) inside `adapters/<adapter_name>/`. Adapter names must match `[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*`.
   207	2. Reference `adapter: <adapter_name>` on any `LlmAgent` in `agent.yaml` or `sub_agents/*.yaml` (for example, `adapter: main_lora` on the root coder agent and `adapter: tool_lora` on a read-only analyzer `AgentTool`).
   208	3. `discover_adapters()` automatically registers all discovered adapters with the vLLM server, right-sizing `enable_lora = bool(adapter_map)`, `max_loras = max(1, len(adapter_map))`, and `max_lora_rank = max(detected_ranks) if detected_ranks else 128` (passed under `--lora-modules name1=path1 name2=path2 ...`), and `resolve_swegemma_adapter` (`src/swegemma/harness/agent_runner.py`) routes requests from each agent to its respective `openai/<adapter_name>` model identifier.
   209	4. **Adapter Size Planning (`< 3 GiB` total budget)**:
   210	   - For a 31B model in `bfloat16` targeting all linear projections (`q_proj`, `k_proj`, `v_proj`, `o_proj`, `gate_proj`, `up_proj`, `down_proj`):
   211	     - **Rank `r = 16`**: `~110–220 MB` per adapter (easily fits 8 specialized adapters within `< 3 GiB`).
   212	     - **Rank `r = 32`**: `~220–450 MB` per adapter (fits 6–8 adapters within `< 3 GiB`).
   213	     - **Rank `r = 64`**: `~450–900 MB` per adapter (fits 3–6 adapters within `< 3 GiB`).
   214	     - **Rank `r = 128` (`max_lora_rank`)**: `~0.9–1.8 GB` per adapter (fits 1–3 high-rank adapters within `< 3 GiB`).
   215	
   216	
   217	---
   218	
   219	## 4. Two-Phase Evaluation Lifecycle
   220	
   221	Every benchmark task (`Task`) is evaluated in **two completely separate sandbox lifecycles** (`Container A` for agent execution and `Container B` for verification).
   222	
   223	```mermaid
   224	sequenceDiagram
   225	    participant Eval as Evaluator (evaluate.py)
   226	    participant ContA as Container A (Agent Sandbox)
   227	    participant Agent as Compiled ADK Agent
   228	    participant ContB as Container B (Verification Sandbox)
   229	
   230	    Eval->>ContA: 1. Start sandbox & setup wheels
   231	    Eval->>ContA: 2. Extract repo snapshot at base_commit (zero future git history)
   232	    Eval->>ContA: 3. Write .git/info/exclude + pip install -e /workspace --no-deps
   233	    Eval->>ContA: 4. Stream cached test site-packages + setup pytest.ini & conftest.py
   234	    Eval->>ContA: 5. git add -A && git commit -m "baseline"
   235	    Eval->>Agent: 6. Start timer (start_agent_session) & send build_agent_prompt()
   236	    loop Until submit_patch(), timeout, budget exhausted, or 3 nudges
   237	        Agent->>ContA: Invoke tools (run_command, read_file, edit_file, etc.)
   238	        ContA-->>Agent: JSON tool response
   239	    end
   240	    Eval->>ContA: 7. Extract agent_patch (submitted_patch or fallback git add -N . && git diff HEAD)
   241	    Eval->>ContA: 8. Stop & clean Container A
   242	    Note over Eval,ContB: If agent_patch is non-empty (or no agent_error), proceed to Phase 2
   243	    Eval->>ContB: 9. Start clean Container B & repeat steps 1–5 (commit "eval_baseline")
   244	    Eval->>ContB: 10. Apply agent_patch (4-pass resilient git apply / patch)
   245	    Eval->>ContB: 11. Reset target test files (git checkout HEAD & git clean -f)
   246	    Eval->>ContB: 12. Apply task.test_patch
   247	    Eval->>ContB: 13. Run hermetic pytest on target test files
   248	    ContB-->>Eval: 14. exit_code == 0 -> resolved = True (score = 1.0)
   249	```
   250	
   251	### 4.1. Sandbox Hardware & Isolation (`ContainerManager` vs `SubprocessManager`)
   252	- **Docker Backend (`--sandbox docker`, default)**:
   253	  - Image: `swebench-sandbox:latest`
   254	  - Network: **`network_mode="none"`** (completely air-gapped; no internet or PyPI access)
   255	  - Memory Limit: **`4g`** (`4 GiB`; processes exceeding this are killed with exit code `137` / `SIGKILL`)
   256	  - CPU Quota: **2 vCPUs** (`cpu_period=100_000`, `cpu_quota=200_000`)
   257	  - Working Directory: `/workspace` (`TEST_TMPDIR=/tmp`)
   258	  - Container Reuse (`reuse_containers=False` default): By default, each phase creates and destroys a fresh container for strict isolation. If warm pooling (`reuse_containers=True`) is explicitly enabled, `/workspace` is completely wiped (`find /workspace -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -exec rm -rf {} +`), temporary patches/stubs in `/tmp` and `/var/tmp` are removed, and the container is returned to an idle pool.
   259	- **Subprocess Backend (`--sandbox subprocess`, used in environments without a Docker daemon)**:
   260	  - Creates an isolated temporary directory `swegemma_sandbox_<id>_` with `workspace/`, `tmp/`, `wheels/`, and an isolated virtual environment `venv/`.
   261	  - Rewrites shell references to `/workspace`, `/tmp`, `/wheels`, and `/usr/local/bin` to the sandbox directory paths and executes commands in a new process group (`start_new_session=True`), terminating the full process group (`os.killpg(..., signal.SIGKILL)`) on command timeout.
   262	
   263	### 4.2. Container Bootstrap Sequence (`container_setup.py`)
   264	Before the agent is invoked (in Container A) and before verification runs (in Container B), the harness performs 7 deterministic setup steps:
   265	
   266	1. **Wheel Staging (`setup_container_wheels`)**: Prepares `/wheels` inside the sandbox.
   267	2. **Snapshot Extraction (`extract_snapshot`)**: Extracts the repository `.tgz` archive (or reconstructs it from a deduplicated base snapshot + binary patch) into `/workspace`, removing any circular symlinks.
   268	   - **Zero Future Git History**: Repository snapshots are built via `git fast-export` up to `base_commit` piped into a fresh `git fast-import` repository on branch `main`. Commits after `base_commit` do not exist in `.git`.
   269	3. **Git Exclude Configuration (`setup_git_exclude`)**: Appends the following patterns to `/workspace/.git/info/exclude` so build/test artifacts never pollute `git diff`:
   270	   ```text
   271	   __pycache__/
   272	   *.pyc
   273	   .pytest_cache/
   274	   *.egg-info/
   275	   build/
   276	   dist/
   277	   .coverage
   278	   ```
   279	4. **Editable Package Installation (`install_editable_package`)**:
   280	   ```bash
   281	   pip install --no-index --find-links=/wheels --no-build-isolation --no-deps -e /workspace 2>/dev/null || true
   282	   ```
   283	5. **Test Dependency Installation (`install_test_dependencies`)**: Streams cached unpacked site-packages (`<20 MB` base wheels and `>=20 MB` workspace-imported wheels) into the container Python environment and runs `/sandbox/setup.py --fast-path <repo>`.
   284	6. **Hermetic Test Configuration (`setup_workspace_test_config`)**: Writes a standardized `/workspace/pytest.ini` (disabling `anyio` and ignoring hardware/device/example directories) and prepends a hermetic test collection hook to `/workspace/conftest.py`.
   285	7. **Baseline Commit (`setup_baseline_commit`)**:
   286	   ```bash
   287	   cd /workspace && git add -A && git commit -m "baseline" --allow-empty -q
   288	   ```
   289	   > [!IMPORTANT]
   290	   > Because `setup_workspace_test_config` runs *before* `setup_baseline_commit`, the harness-generated `/workspace/pytest.ini` and `/workspace/conftest.py` are committed into `HEAD` (`baseline`). Do **not** delete or modify `/workspace/pytest.ini` or `/workspace/conftest.py` unless required by the task, as changes to them will appear in `git diff HEAD`.
   291	
   292	---
   293	
   294	## 5. Harness-to-Agent Interaction Protocol
   295	
   296	### 5.1. Session State Variables
   297	When `run_agent_sandbox` creates the ADK session (`app_name='swegemma_eval'`, `user_id='eval_user'`), it populates `session.state` with:
   298	- `problem_description`: `task.problem_statement` (`str`)
   299	- `hints`: `task.hints_text.strip()` (`str`, only present if `task.hints_text` is non-empty)
   300	
   301	Because Google ADK automatically interpolates `{variable_name}` placeholders in `LlmAgent.instruction` from `session.state`, your `agent.yaml` or sub-agent instructions can reference `{problem_description}` Directly.
   302	
   303	### 5.2. Initial User Prompt (`build_agent_prompt`)
   304	The harness sends a structured initial user message to the root agent containing up to 7 sections (automatically filtering tool-specific rules and sections against `_extract_declared_tools(agent)` so undeclared tools are never advertised):
   305	
   306	1. **Task Header & Problem Statement**:
   307	   ```markdown
   308	   You are evaluating a software engineering task for repository {task.repo}.
   309	
   310	   Problem Statement:
   311	   {task.problem_statement}
   312	   ```
   313	2. **Hints** *(included if `task.hints_text` is non-empty)*:
   314	   ```markdown
   315	   ## Hints:
   316	   {task.hints_text}
   317	   ```
   318	3. **Task Budget** *(lists all active limits from `EvalConfig.budget`)*:
   319	   ```markdown
   320	   ## Task Budget (Session terminates when any budget is exhausted)
   321	   - Time allowance: 60.0 minutes
   322	   - Tool calls allowance: <N> calls
   323	   - Max loop iterations: <N> turns
   324	   - Cost budget: $<X.XX> USD
   325	   ```
   326	4. **Execution Environment Rules** *(the `read_file` line/character limits are omitted if `read_file` is not declared in the agent hierarchy)*:
   327	   ```markdown
   328	   ## Execution Environment Rules
   329	   - Single command timeout: 300 seconds (commands exceeding this fail without ending the session)
   330	   - Command output limit: 5000 characters
   331	   - File view limit: 150 lines per read_file call
   332	   - File character limit: 10000 characters per read_file call
   333	   - Environment is offline (no network/PyPI access). All repository and test dependencies are ALREADY pre-installed. Do NOT attempt to run pip install or download packages.
   334	   ```
   335	5. **Standard Instructions (`0`–`5`)**:
   336	   - Directs the agent to work strictly under `/workspace`, inspect existing conventions, verify its implementation using targeted tests or inline assertions before submitting, call `submit_patch` once complete, and return a final text completion response.
   337	   - *Note on in-sandbox testing*: By default, `enable_sandbox_testing = True` is active in `EvalConfig` and `agent_runner.py`, so `pytest` and `unittest` are available inside Container A and Instruction `3` reads: `"3. Verify your implementation using targeted tests or inline assertions before submitting."` (If `enable_sandbox_testing` is explicitly set to `False`, the harness masks `pytest` and `unittest` inside Container A and adjusts Instruction `3` to advise inline `python3 -c "..."` assertions).
   338	6. **Code Intelligence Tools** *(conditionally appended when pre-computed graph `.json` and embedding `.npz` files >100 bytes exist for `task.repo`, listing only the graph/embedding tools actually declared across the agent hierarchy)*:
   339	   ```markdown
   340	   ## Code Intelligence Tools
   341	   This repository has pre-built code graph and embedding data indexed by dotted symbol path (e.g. `pkg.module.Class.method`). Use these tools for fast, targeted navigation:
   342	   - `search_similar_code(query)`: Find functions/classes with similar precomputed embeddings given a symbol name (e.g. `pkg.module.Class.method`, `Class.method`, or `func_name`).
   343	   - `get_code_neighbors(node)`: Find callers and callees of a symbol node (e.g. `pkg.module.Class.method` or `Class.method`).
   344	   - `get_code_subgraph(nodes)`: Get the induced call subgraph connecting a list of symbol nodes.
   345	   ```
   346	7. **Workspace Layout**:
   347	   Captures up to 200 breadth-first-selected, hierarchically sorted entries of `find . -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 3` inside `/workspace` (excluding hidden files/directories, `__pycache__`, `*.pyc`, `*.egg-info`, and pruning non-package bulk directories such as `docs`, `docs_src`, `tests`, `examples`, and `benchmarks` below depth 1).
   348	
   349	### 5.3. Multi-Turn Loop, Tool Error Recovery, Continuation Nudges & Termination
   350	The harness drives the root agent in an outer `while True:` loop (`agent_runner.py`):
   351	- **Tool Error Recovery (`ToolErrorPlugin` & Skill Argument Coercion)**:
   352	  - `ToolErrorPlugin` registers `on_tool_error_callback` on the ADK `App` so that undeclared/hallucinated tool calls or runtime tool exceptions are caught and returned to the model as structured error dictionaries (`{"status": "error", "error_type": "<ExcName>", "error": "<Message>"}`) instead of aborting the task.
   353	  - Both `ToolErrorPlugin.before_tool_callback` and `_wrap_skill_toolset_tools` automatically coerce single-element list strings (`["name"]` $\to$ `"name"`) for `skill_name` and `file_path` on ADK `SkillToolset` tools (`load_skill`, `load_skill_resource`, `run_skill_script`).
   354	- **Immediate Termination on `submit_patch()`**:
   355	  - During `runner.run_async(...)`, if the agent calls `submit_patch()`, `context.patch_submitted` becomes `True`.
   356	  - As soon as the agent emits a final text response (`is_final_response()`) or finishes the current `run_async` turn with `context.patch_submitted == True`, **the harness immediately breaks out of the agent loop** and proceeds to patch extraction and Phase 2 verification.
   357	- **Continuation Nudge Mechanism (`max_nudges = 3`)**:
   358	  - If an ADK turn ends (`runner.run_async` returns) **without** `submit_patch()` having been called—for instance, because the model emitted a text-only response or hit `max_output_tokens`—and budgets are still available:
   359	    - If the turn executed at least one valid tool call (`turn_has_tool_call == True`), `consecutive_nudges` resets to `0`.
   360	    - Otherwise, `consecutive_nudges` increments (`1`, `2`, `3`). If `consecutive_nudges > 3`, the loop terminates.
   361	    - The harness sends one of three targeted user continuation messages based on how the turn ended:
   362	
   363	| Condition Detected on Turn Exit | Injected Continuation User Message (`nudge_prompt`) |
   364	| :--- | :--- |
   365	| **Unclosed `<\|tool_call>` tag** in `last_assistant_text` | `"Your previous response reached the token limit before the tool call finished closing (<\|tool_call\|> was cut off). Do NOT repeat your prior reasoning in thought—emit your next tool call immediately, and if calling edit_file or write_file, split the change into smaller incremental edits."` |
   366	| **`finish_reason` contains `MAX_TOKENS` or `LENGTH`** | `"Your previous response reached the token limit while thinking before a tool call was completed. Do NOT repeat your analysis in thought—keep reasoning under a few sentences and emit your next tool call immediately, or call submit_patch when you have completed and verified your changes."` |
   367	| **Normal stop without calling `submit_patch`** | `"Please continue your work using the available tools, or call submit_patch when you have completed and verified your changes."` |
   368	
   369	---
   370	
   371	## 6. Built-In Tools Reference (`swegemma.tools`)
   372	
   373	`SwegemmaContext.create_tools()` registers **9 tools** in `ToolRegistry`. Your `agent.yaml` (and any sub-agent YAML) can attach any subset of these tools by name under `tools:`.
   374	
   375	All `@budget_gated` tools return a JSON string with either:
   376	- **Success**: `{"status": "ok", ...}`
   377	- **Error**: `{"status": "error", "error_type": "<Type>", "error_message": "<Message>", "details": {...}}`
   378	
   379	If `budget.tool_calls >= 20` and `remaining_tool_calls <= 10`, every tool response automatically includes an extra field:
   380	```json
   381	"budget_warning": "Only X tool call(s) remaining (Y/Z used). Finalize your edits and call submit_patch soon."
   382	```
   383	
   384	### 6.1. Execution & Lifecycle Tools (`swegemma/tools/execution.py`)
   385	
   386	#### 1. `run_command(command: str) -> str`
   387	Executes a shell command in `/bin/bash -c` inside `/workspace`.
   388	- **Counts toward `tool_calls` budget**: **Yes** (`@budget_gated`).
   389	- **Timeout**: `min(command_timeout_seconds [300s], max(5, int(remaining_time_seconds)))`. If a command times out, it returns `error_type: "TimeoutExceeded"` **without ending the agent session** (unless the overall session time budget has also expired).
   390	- **Output Truncation**: Both `stdout` and `stderr` are truncated to `max_stdout_chars` (**`5,000` characters** default).
   391	- **Returns**:
   392	  - If `exit_code == 0`:
   393	    ```json
   394	    {"status": "ok", "stdout": "...", "stderr": "...", "exit_code": 0}
   395	    ```
   396	  - If `exit_code != 0`:
   397	    ```json
   398	    {
   399	      "status": "error",
   400	      "error_type": "CommandError",
   401	      "error_message": "...",
   402	      "details": {"stdout": "...", "stderr": "...", "exit_code": 1}
   403	    }
   404	    ```
   405	
   406	#### 2. `submit_patch() -> str`
   407	Stages untracked file intents (`git add -N .`), captures `git diff --binary _swegemma_baseline 2>/dev/null || git diff --binary HEAD` from `/workspace`, stores it in `ctx.submitted_patch`, and sets `ctx.patch_submitted = True`.
   408	- **Counts toward `tool_calls` budget**: **No** (`@budget_gated(count_tool_call=False)`). Can still be called even when `tool_calls_used == budget.tool_calls` (as long as session wall-clock time remains).
   409	- **Session Effect**: Marks the task patch as submitted; once the agent finishes its response for the current turn, the harness exits the agent loop.
   410	- **Returns**:
   411	  ```json
   412	  {"status": "ok", "patch_size": 1420, "files_changed": 2}
   413	  ```
   414	
   415	#### 3. `get_status() -> str`
   416	Returns live budget consumption and patch status.
   417	- **Counts toward `tool_calls` budget**: **No** (`@budget_gated(count_tool_call=False)`).
   418	- **Returns**:
   419	  ```json
   420	  {
   421	    "status": "ok",
   422	    "tool_calls_used": 12,
   423	    "patch_submitted": false,
   424	    "patch_size": 0,
   425	    "tool_calls_remaining": 38,
   426	    "max_tool_calls": 50,
   427	    "time_seconds_remaining": 3120.4,
   428	    "max_time_minutes": 60.0,
   429	    "agent_elapsed_seconds": 479.6,
   430	    "max_turns": 500,
   431	    "command_timeout_seconds": 300
   432	  }
   433	  ```
   434	
   435	---
   436	
   437	### 6.2. Workspace File Tools (`swegemma/tools/workspace.py`)
   438	
   439	All file paths are resolved relative to `/workspace` via `_resolve_workspace_path(filepath)`. Leading `/` or `/workspace/` prefixes are stripped automatically (`"/workspace/src/app.py"` and `"src/app.py"` both resolve to `/workspace/src/app.py`). Any path containing `..` traversal raises a `ValidationError`. String-encoded integer and boolean arguments emitted by LLMs (e.g., `"10"` for `start_line` or `"true"` for `allow_multiple`) are automatically coerced to their target Python types.
   440	
   441	#### 4. `read_file(filepath: str, start_line: int | None = None, end_line: int | None = None) -> str`
   442	Reads a file from `/workspace` with 1-indexed inclusive line slicing.
   443	- **Counts toward `tool_calls` budget**: **Yes** (`@budget_gated`).
   444	- **Dual Truncation Cap**: Output is capped at **`150` lines** (`max_file_lines`) AND **`10,000` characters** (`max_file_chars`). If either limit is reached, `is_truncated` is set to `true` and `end_line` reflects the last complete line returned.
   445	- **Returns**:
   446	  ```json
   447	  {
   448	    "status": "ok",
   449	    "filepath": "src/module.py",
   450	    "content": "...",
   451	    "start_line": 1,
   452	    "end_line": 150,
   453	    "total_lines": 420,
   454	    "is_truncated": true
   455	  }
   456	  ```
   457	
   458	#### 5. `edit_file(filepath: str, old_string: str, new_string: str, allow_multiple: bool = False) -> str`
   459	Replaces `old_string` with `new_string` in an existing non-empty file inside `/workspace` using `adk-eval-core`'s **3-tier resilient matching engine** (`apply_replacement`):
   460	- **Counts toward `tool_calls` budget**: **Yes** (`@budget_gated`).
   461	- **3-Tier Matching Algorithm**:
   462	  1. **`exact`**: Exact character-for-character substring match (after normalizing `\r\n` $\to$ `\n`).
   463	  2. **`flexible`**: Line-by-line match after stripping leading/trailing whitespace on each line. Automatically re-indents `new_string` to preserve the matched block's baseline indentation.
   464	  3. **`regex`**: Tokenizes `old_string` around code delimiters `( ) : [ ] { } > = <` and joins tokens with flexible whitespace (`\s*`), matching across minor line-wrapping or spacing discrepancies.
   465	- **Uniqueness Check**: If `old_string` matches more than 1 occurrence and `allow_multiple=False`, returns `error_type: "FileEditError"` without modifying the file. Also returns `FileEditError` if the file does not exist, the file is empty (`0` bytes), or `old_string` is empty (`""`).
   466	- **Returns** (`diff` is truncated at `max_stdout_chars = 5,000` chars):
   467	  ```json
   468	  {
   469	    "status": "ok",
   470	    "filepath": "src/module.py",
   471	    "occurrences": 1,
   472	    "strategy": "exact",
   473	    "diff": "--- a/src/module.py\n+++ b/src/module.py\n...",
   474	    "is_truncated": false
   475	  }
   476	  ```
   477	
   478	#### 6. `write_file(filepath: str, content: str) -> str`
   479	Creates or overwrites a file at `/workspace/<filepath>`, automatically creating parent directories (`mkdir -p`).
   480	- **Counts toward `tool_calls` budget**: **Yes** (`@budget_gated`).
   481	- **Returns**:
   482	  ```json
   483	  {"status": "ok", "filepath": "tests/repro.py", "size": 342}
   484	  ```
   485	
   486	---
   487	
   488	### 6.3. Code Intelligence Graph Tools (`swegemma/tools/graph.py`)
   489	
   490	When pre-computed AST/dependency graphs (`data/graphs/<repo>.json`) and node embeddings (`data/embeddings/<repo>.npz`) are available for a repository, these three tools query the in-memory NetworkX `CustomMultiDiGraph` (automatically coercing string-encoded integers for `max_neighbors`/`k` and JSON or comma-separated strings for `nodes`):
   491	
   492	#### 7. `get_code_neighbors(node: str, edge_type: str | None = None, max_neighbors: int = 50) -> str`
   493	Finds incoming and outgoing neighbors of a symbol (`node`) in the repository call/dependency graph.
   494	- **Counts toward `tool_calls` budget**: **Yes** (`@budget_gated`).
   495	- **Symbol Resolution**: `resolve_node_name` resolves short names (e.g., `"FastAPI.get"` or `"Request"`) via 4 tiers: exact match $\to$ `.` / `;` / `/` suffix match $\to$ case-insensitive match $\to$ substring match.
   496	- **`edge_type`**: Optional case-insensitive filter (e.g., `"calls"`).
   497	- **Output Truncation**: Neighbor names are capped to `max_stdout_chars` (**`5,000` characters** default), setting `is_truncated: true` if truncated.
   498	- **Returns**:
   499	  ```json
   500	  {"status": "ok", "node": "fastapi.applications.FastAPI", "neighbors": ["..."], "count": 18, "is_truncated": false}
   501	  ```
   502	
   503	#### 8. `search_similar_code(query: str, k: int = 10) -> str`
   504	Finds top-`k` graph nodes with highest cosine similarity to `query` in the pre-computed `.npz` embedding archive.
   505	- **Counts toward `tool_calls` budget**: **Yes** (`@budget_gated`).
   506	- **Query Resolution Note**: Because the sandbox runs offline without a live neural embedding server, `search_similar_code` resolves `query` against node keys/suffixes stored in the graph and `.npz` dictionary and computes cosine similarity across all other nodes in the repository (with a lexical token-overlap fallback if `query` is a multi-word keyword string). Pass a dotted symbol path or short symbol name (e.g., `"pkg.module.Class.method"`, `"Class.method"`, or `"parse_header"`).
   507	- **Output Truncation**: Total `code` characters across all `k` returned nodes are capped at `max_stdout_chars` (**`5,000` characters** default), dividing the budget across the `k` results with unused characters from shorter snippets rolling over to larger nodes, and setting `is_truncated: true` when any snippet is truncated.
   508	- **Returns**:
   509	  ```json
   510	  {
   511	    "status": "ok",
   512	    "query": "HTTPConnection",
   513	    "results": [{"node_name": "...", "code": "...", "similarity": 0.9412}],
   514	    "count": 10,
   515	    "is_truncated": false
   516	  }
   517	  ```
   518	
   519	#### 9. `get_code_subgraph(nodes: list[str]) -> str`
   520	Extracts the induced subgraph (all nodes and interconnecting edges) for a list of symbols.
   521	- **Counts toward `tool_calls` budget**: **Yes** (`@budget_gated`).
   522	- **Output Truncation**: Returned `nodes` and `edges` lists are capped to `max_stdout_chars` (**`5,000` characters** default), setting `is_truncated: true` if truncated.
   523	- **Returns**:
   524	  ```json
   525	  {
   526	    "status": "ok",
   527	    "nodes": ["node_a", "node_b"],
   528	    "edges": [{"from": "node_a", "to": "node_b", "type": "calls"}],
   529	    "node_count": 2,
   530	    "edge_count": 1,
   531	    "is_truncated": false
   532	  }
   533	  ```
   534	
   535	
   536	---
   537	
   538	## 7. Budgets, Operational Limits & Context Management
   539	
   540	### 7.1. Default Budgets (`EvaluationBudget`), `eval_config.yaml` & Harness Limits (`HarnessLimits`)
   541	
   542	Participants can customize per-task budgets in their submission's `eval_config.yaml` (under the `evaluation:` key), which `scripts/inference.py` loads via `load_submission_eval_config()`:
   543	
   544	```yaml
   545	evaluation:
   546	  timeout_seconds: 300
   547	  max_tool_calls: 10
   548	  max_time_minutes: 1
   549	  max_turns: 50
   550	```
   551	
   552	| Parameter | `EvalConfig` Path | `swegemma` / `inference.py` Default | `eval_config.yaml` Key | Environment Variable Override (`scripts/inference.py`) |
   553	| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
   554	| **Session Wall-Clock Time** | `config.budget.time_minutes` | `60.0` min (`1` min in sample submission) | `evaluation.max_time_minutes` | `SWE_MAX_TIME_MINUTES` |
   555	| **Max Tool Calls** | `config.budget.tool_calls` | `100` in `inference.py` / `None` in CLI (`10` in sample submission) | `evaluation.max_tool_calls` | `SWE_MAX_TOOL_CALLS` |
   556	| **Max LLM Reasoning Turns** | `config.budget.turns` | `500` turns (`None` $\to$ `500`; `50` in sample submission) | `evaluation.max_turns` | `SWE_MAX_TURNS` / `SWE_MAX_LOOP_ITERATIONS` |
   557	| **Max Monetary Cost** | `config.budget.cost_usd` | `None` | — | — |
   558	| **Max Total Tokens** | `config.budget.total_tokens` | `None` | — | — |
   559	| **Single Command Timeout (Phase 1)** | `config.harness.command_timeout_seconds` | `300` sec | `evaluation.timeout_seconds` | `SWE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` |
   560	| **Verification `pytest` Timeout (Phase 2)** | `config.harness.verification_timeout_seconds` | `300` sec | — *(fixed at `300` sec on scorer)* | `SWE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` (`metric.py`) |
   561	| **Max Command / Diff / Graph Output** | `config.harness.max_stdout_chars` | `5,000` chars | — | — |
   562	| **Max Lines per `read_file`** | `config.harness.max_file_lines` | `150` lines | — | — |
   563	| **Max Chars per `read_file`** | `config.harness.max_file_chars` | `10,000` chars | — | — |
   564	
   565	> [!TIP]
   566	> **Container Setup Is Excluded from Agent Time Budget**: `SwegemmaContext` records `task_start_time` at creation, but `agent_start_time` is started via `context.start_agent_session()` *only after* container creation, wheel installation, and snapshot extraction finish. Your `time_minutes` budget measures strictly the time spent inside the agent loop.
   567	
   568	### 7.2. Automatic Context Window Compaction & Prefix Caching
   569	To prevent long debugging trajectories from overflowing the 32,768-token context window on vLLM, `swegemma` configures Google ADK's context management on the `App` instance (`scripts/inference.py`):
   570	- **Events Compaction (`EventsCompactionConfig`)**:
   571	  - `compaction_interval = 15`
   572	  - `overlap_size = 2`
   573	  - `token_threshold = 14,336`
   574	  - `event_retention_size = 5`
   575	- **Context Caching (`ContextCacheConfig`)**:
   576	  - `min_tokens = 2,048`, `ttl_seconds = 1,800`, `cache_intervals = 10`
   577	- **Transient Model Error Retries (`ModelRetryPlugin`)**:
   578	  - Automatically intercepts transient LLM/proxy errors (`429`, `500`, `502`, `503`, `504`, `RateLimitError`, `APIConnectionError`, `APITimeoutError`) and retries up to `5` times with exponential backoff (`2.0s` initial delay, `2.0x` multiplier, max `60.0s`, $\pm 20\%$ jitter).
   579	
   580	---
   581	
   582	## 8. Patch Extraction & Phase 2 Verification (`verify_task`)
   583	
   584	### 8.1. How Patches Are Extracted (`submit_patch` & Automatic Fallback)
   585	Both explicit `submit_patch()` calls and the harness fallback run the exact same git commands inside `/workspace` in Container A:
   586	```bash
   587	cd /workspace && git add -N .
   588	cd /workspace && (git diff --binary _swegemma_baseline 2>/dev/null || git diff --binary HEAD)
   589	```
   590	1. **Why `git add -N .` Matters**: `--intent-to-add` registers any newly created untracked files in the git index with empty content so that **`git diff` includes both modifications to existing tracked files AND newly created files**, while still ignoring everything listed in `/workspace/.git/info/exclude` (`__pycache__/`, `*.pyc`, `.pytest_cache/`, `*.egg-info/`, `build/`, `dist/`, `.coverage`).
   591	2. **Protected Test & Config File Stripping (`strip_protected_files_from_patch`)**: Both `submit_patch()` and the unsubmitted-diff fallback strip any diff hunks touching protected test files (`test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, or `.py` files under `tests/`, `test/`, `testing/`) or runner configuration files (`conftest.py`, `pytest.ini`, `pyproject.toml`, `tox.ini`, `setup.cfg`, `.pytest.ini`, `sitecustomize.py`, `usercustomize.py`, `_swegemma_stubs.py`, `*.pth`) so temporary reproduction tests or runner config edits never pollute `agent_patch`.
   592	3. **Automatic Unsubmitted Patch Recovery (`agent_runner.py`, lines 760–805)**:
   593	   If an agent finishes, hits `max_nudges`, exhausts `tool_calls` or `turns`, or times out **without ever calling `submit_patch()`**, the harness automatically runs `git add -N .`, `git diff --binary _swegemma_baseline`, and `strip_protected_files_from_patch` before tearing down Container A.
   594	   - If the working tree contains non-empty modifications to non-protected files (`agent_patch != ""`), `Evaluator.evaluate_task` (`evaluate.py`) and `scripts/inference.py` still record `agent_patch` in `submission.parquet` (`[id, prediction]`) so it is evaluated in Phase 2 verification!
   595	
   596	### 8.2. Phase 2 Verification in Container B (`harness/verification.py` & `scripts/metric.py`)
   597	During Stage 2 scoring (`score(solution, submission, row_id_column_name)` in `scripts/metric.py`), when `agent_patch` (`prediction`) is non-empty, `verify_task` executes in a fresh **Container B**:
   598	1. **Fresh Baseline Setup**: Extracts the original task snapshot at `base_commit`, configures synthetic hardware stubs and `.git/info/exclude`, installs `/workspace` in editable mode, installs cached test dependencies, writes `/workspace/pytest.ini` and `/workspace/conftest.py`, and creates the `eval_baseline` commit (`HEAD`).
   599	2. **Protected File Stripping & 4-Pass Resilient Patch Application (`strip_protected_files_from_patch` & `apply_patch_in_container`)**:
   600	   Strips any diff sections targeting protected test or runner configuration files (`strip_protected_files_from_patch`) and applies the remaining `agent_patch` using four fallback passes until one succeeds:
   601	   - **Pass 1**: `git apply --unsafe-paths` (`-p1`), then `-3` (three-way merge), then `--ignore-space-change --ignore-whitespace`, then `--recount`.
   602	   - **Pass 2**: Symlink-normalized `git apply --unsafe-paths` (`-p1`) (resolving symlinked directory prefixes inside `/workspace`).
   603	   - **Pass 3**: Prefixless `-p0` `git apply --unsafe-paths`.
   604	   - **Pass 4**: Non-interactive GNU `patch -p1` / `patch -p0` (`--batch --forward` and `-l`), gated by `--dry-run`.
   605	   - If all 4 passes fail (`exit_code != 0`), the task immediately fails with `resolved = False` (`error = "Failed to apply agent patch: ..."`).
   606	3. **Anti-Tampering Test & Config Reset (`_is_protected_test_or_config_path`)**:
   607	   The harness identifies all file paths referenced in `task.test_patch` **plus** any protected test files (`test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, or `.py` files under `tests/`, `test/`, `testing/`) and test runner configuration files (`conftest.py`, `pytest.ini`, `pyproject.toml`, `tox.ini`, `setup.cfg`, `.pytest.ini`, `sitecustomize.py`, `usercustomize.py`, `_swegemma_stubs.py`, `*.pth`) touched by `agent_patch`, and forcefully resets them to `eval_baseline` (`HEAD`) before applying `task.test_patch` (filtering tracked pathspecs via `git ls-tree` so missing pathspecs do not abort `git checkout`):
   608	   ```bash
   609	   cd /workspace && tracked=$(git ls-tree -r --name-only HEAD -- <files_to_reset> 2>/dev/null) \
   610	     && [ -n "$tracked" ] && git ls-tree -r -z --name-only HEAD -- <files_to_reset> \
   611	     | xargs -0 git checkout HEAD -- 2>/dev/null || true
   612	   cd /workspace && git clean -f -- <files_to_reset> 2>/dev/null || true
   613	   ```
   614	   > [!CAUTION]
   615	   > Any modifications your agent makes to test files or runner configurations (`conftest.py`, `pytest.ini`, `pyproject.toml`, etc.) are **stripped and discarded** before `task.test_patch` is applied. Your agent must fix the underlying library code under `/workspace`, not alter test files or test runner hooks.
   616	4. **Apply `task.test_patch` & Run Hermetic `pytest` with JUnit XML**:
   617	   The harness applies `task.test_patch`, refreshes test dependencies and `pytest.ini`/`conftest.py`, and runs:
   618	   ```bash
   619	   cd /workspace && PYTHONSAFEPATH=1 PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1 python3 -s -m pytest <pytest_targets> \
   620	     --junitxml=/tmp/_swegemma_junit_<id>.xml \
   621	     -p no:anyio -o timeout=0 \
   622	     -o python_classes="Test* *Test" -q
   623	   ```
   624	5. **Resolution Criterion (`resolved`), JUnit XML Validation & Competition Score**:
   625	   - A task is marked **`resolved = True`** (`score = 1.0`) **if and only if**:
   626	     1. `pytest` exits with return code `0` (`test_res.exit_code == 0`), **and**
   627	     2. `_validate_junit_xml` confirms the JUnit XML report exists, has `passed_tests > 0`, `failures == 0`, `errors == 0`, and every required test node (`FAIL_TO_PASS`, `PASS_TO_PASS`, or test functions extracted from `test_patch`) explicitly passed without being skipped.
   628	   - The overall competition metric returned by `score()` in `scripts/metric.py` is the **Resolution Rate**:
   629	     $$\text{Resolution Rate} = \frac{\text{Number of Resolved Tasks}}{\text{Total Tasks in Evaluation Split}} \in [0.0, 1.0]$$
   630	
   631	---
   632	
   633	## 9. Local CLI Usage & Output Artifacts
   634	
   635	### 9.1. Running Evaluations Locally (`swegemma eval`)
   636	You can evaluate any submission directory locally against the published training tasks using the `swegemma` CLI:
   637	
   638	```bash
   639	swegemma eval \
   640	  --tasks tasks.jsonl \
   641	  --snapshots-dir snapshots \
   642	  --submission-dir sample_submission \
   643	  --results-dir results/run_01 \
   644	  --sandbox docker \
   645	  --max-tool-calls 50 \
   646	  --max-time-minutes 30 \
   647	  --concurrency 2 \
   648	  --display auto
   649	```
   650	
   651	Key CLI flags (`swegemma eval`):
   652	- `--task-id <id>` / `--task-ids <id1> <id2>`: Evaluate specific `instance_id`(s) for rapid debugging.
   653	- `--sandbox {docker,subprocess}`: Choose Docker containers (default) or local subprocess isolation.
   654	- `--timeout-seconds <sec>`: Per-command timeout in seconds for Phase 1 agent tool calls (default `300`).
   655	- `--verification-timeout-seconds <sec>`: Timeout in seconds for Phase 2 `pytest` verification runs (default `300`).
   656	- `--concurrency <N>`: Evaluate `N` tasks in parallel (automatically activates the multi-slot `dashboard` UI).
   657	- `--shard-index <k> --num-shards <M>`: Run deterministic shard `k` of `M` (`task_idx % M == k`).
   658	- `--models-yaml <path>`: Custom `models.yaml` file mapping model aliases and token pricing.
   659	- `--skip-agent-patch`: Bypass Phase 1 (`agent_patch = ""`) to verify baseline test failure behavior in Phase 2.
   660	
   661	
   662	### 9.2. Results Directory Artifacts (`--results-dir`)
   663	Every evaluation run writes incremental, crash-safe outputs to `--results-dir`:
   664	
   665	```text
   666	results/run_01/
   667	├── summary.json                        # Aggregate resolution_rate, resolved count, per-repo breakdown, and errors
   668	├── task_results.jsonl                  # Append-only JSONL (one line per finished task with metrics & exit codes)
   669	├── patches/
   670	│   └── <instance_id>.patch             # Exact unified diff extracted from Container A
   671	├── test_outputs/
   672	│   └── <instance_id>.log               # Complete STDOUT/STDERR from Phase 2 pytest verification in Container B
   673	├── traces/
   674	│   └── trace_<instance_id>.json        # Full SessionTrace (ATIF-compatible steps, thoughts, tool calls, token usage)
   675	└── logs/
   676	    └── <instance_id>.log               # Rich formatted transcript of the Phase 1 agent session
   677	```
   678	
   679	---
   680	
   681	## 10. Competitor Best Practices & Important Gotchas
   682	
   683	1. **Keep `edit_file` Payloads Focused to Avoid `<|tool_call>` Truncation**:
   684	   When an LLM generates a massive thought block followed by a large `edit_file` or `write_file` tool call that hits `max_output_tokens`, the `<|tool_call>` tag gets cut off before closing. Set a reasonable `thinking_budget` (e.g., `4096` with `max_output_tokens: 16384`) and instruct your agent to apply incremental edits.
   685	2. **Do Not Modify `/workspace/pytest.ini` or `/workspace/conftest.py`**:
   686	   The harness writes these two files and commits them into the `baseline` commit before your agent starts. If your agent deletes or modifies them, those diffs will be included in `agent_patch`.
   687	3. **Clean Up Temporary Reproduction Scripts Before `submit_patch()` (or Put Them in `/tmp`)**:
   688	   Because `submit_patch()` runs `git add -N . && git diff HEAD` inside `/workspace`, any untracked reproduction script created inside `/workspace` (e.g., `/workspace/repro.py`) will be included in your patch! Put scratch test scripts in **`/tmp/repro.py`** via `run_command`, or delete them from `/workspace` before calling `submit_patch()`.
   689	4. **Use `AgentTool` (`skip_summarization: true`) to Isolate Context Window Usage**:
   690	   File exploration and graph traversal can quickly consume tokens. Delegating code search to a read-only sub-agent wrapped as an `agent_tool` (like `sub_agents/code_analyzer.yaml` in the starter kit) keeps intermediate `read_file` outputs out of the root coder agent's main context history.
   691	5. **Always Call `submit_patch()` Explicitly, Know That It Is Free, and Do It Last**:
   692	   `submit_patch()` does not count toward `tool_calls` (`count_tool_call=False`), and `get_status()` is also completely free. However, once a turn completes with `patch_submitted == True`, the harness immediately terminates the agent loop. Always verify your changes first, clean up any scratch files in `/workspace`, and call `submit_patch()` as your final tool action.

